<chapter
 id="lecture08-10">
#title("Example: Estimating Pi")

#para("\

Generally speaking, the Monte Carlo method refers to a technique that
attempts to find an approximate solution to a numerical problem by
making use of randomly generated inputs. I would like to present as
follows an implementation based on such a technique that approximates
the value of the constant pi (the ratio of the circumference of circle
over its diameter).

")

#para("\

The meaning of the following declarations should be clear (the point is a flat
tuple, and a list of points is a linear list, which has to be freed):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
\#define N 1024
stadef N = 1024
//
typedef
point = @(double, double)
//
vtypedef points = List0_vt(point)
//
')

Given a grid of the dimension N by N, there are N#sup("2") unit
squares in it. For each point on the grid, the area of the unit disk
centered at the point equals pi (as the radius of the unit disk is 1).
Given a unit contained in the grid, the probability of a randomly
chosen point on the grid residing within the disk is clearly
pi/N#sup("2").

")

#para("\

Assume that there are K points on the grid. A randomly chosen point
generates one hit if it resides within the unit disk centered at one
point among the K points. Accumulatively, a randomly chosen point can
generate n hits if it resides within the unit disks centered at n
points among the K points.

")

#para("\

Let create a matrix of the dimension N by N for storing lists of points. The
matrix is linear (its cells are linear lists) and it is created by
#dyncode("matrixptr_tabulate"), whose template parameter gives the empty
list for each cell:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// The points that were chosen up to now are stored in the lists of
// the grid: each list is linear and so is the grid, which is a
// matrix of lists (each list is empty to begin with).
//
implement
matrix_tabulate$fopr<points>(i, j) = list_vt_nil()
//
')

We are to play N#sup("2") rounds of experiment.  At round K for each K
between 1 and N#sup("2"), there are already K-1 points on the grid
chosen randomly in the previous rounds.  We choose a new point on the
grid randomly for round K and records the number of hits it generates
(with respect to the K-1 points on the grid). We then add this new
point to the list stored at the matrix cell (i, j), where i and j are
the integer parts of the x-coordinate and y-coordinate, respectively.

")

#para("\

The following functions are for computing the number of hits generated
by a point with respect to a collection of points. In the GC era, the lists of
points and the lists of lists of points were all shared and read at will;
here a list stored in a cell is linear, and it is taken out of the cell for
a moment (the cell holds the empty list in the meantime) and put back after
having been read, which is done by exchanging the content of the cell with a
variable. A function that compares two points, one that counts the hits generated
by a point with respect to a list of points, and one that does it with
respect to the list stored in a cell are as follows:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// the square of the distance (no need of the square root)
fun
dist2(p1: point, p2: point): double = let
  val dx = p1.0 - p2.0 and dy = p1.1 - p2.1
in
  dx*dx + dy*dy
end // end of [dist2]
//
fun
randfloat(): double = drand48()
//

//
fun
hit_point_point
(
p1: point, p2: point
) : int =
(
if dist2(p1, p2) <= 1.0 then 1 else 0
)
//
fun
hit_point_points
(
p0: point, ps: !points
) : int = let
  var fopr =
  lam@(res: int, p1: point): int => res + hit_point_point(p0, p1)
in
  list_foldleft<int><point>(ps, 0, fopr)
end // end of [hit_point_points]
//

//
// A grid cell holds a linear list of points. In order to read a list,
// it is taken out of the cell (and the cell holds the empty list for a
// moment), and it is put back after the reading.
//
fun
hit_point_cell
(
p0: point, grid: !matrixptr(points, N, N), i: int, j: int
) : int = let
//
val i = g1ofg0(i) and j = g1ofg0(j)
//
in
//
if
(i >= 0)
then if
(i < N)
then if
(j >= 0)
then if
(j < N)
then let
  var ps: points = list_vt_nil()
  val () = matrixptr_exch_at_int(grid, i, N, j, ps)
  val nhit = hit_point_points(p0, ps)
  val () = matrixptr_exch_at_int(grid, i, N, j, ps)
  val-~list_vt_nil() = ps
in
  nhit
end // end of [then]
else 0 else 0 else 0 else 0 // the position is not valid
//
end // end of [hit_point_cell]
//
')

")

#para("\

A unit square in the grid is located at (i, j) if the lower-left
corner of the unit square is at the point (i, j).  Two unit squares
are neighbors if they locate at (i1, j1) and (i2, j2) such that
abs(i1-i2) and abs(j1-j2) are at most 1, where abs refers to the
absolute value function (on integers). Given a point inside the unit
square located at (i, j), the point can only generate hits with
respect to those residing inside the neighbors of the unit square.
Given a unit square at (i, j), the following function computes the number of the hits generated by a
point with respect to the entire neighbors of the unit square (a list of the
cells of the neighbors is not built any more: it would be a list of
borrowed lists):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// Given a unit square at (i, j), the entire neighbors of the unit square
// are the 9 unit squares at (i+di, j+dj) for di and dj in {-1, 0, 1}.
//
fun
hit_point_neighbors
(
p0: point
, grid: !matrixptr(points, N, N), i: int, j: int
) : int = let
//
fun
loop
(grid: !matrixptr(points, N, N), k: int, res: int): int =
if k >= 9
  then res
  else loop(grid, k+1, res + hit_point_cell(p0, grid, i + k/3 - 1, j + k%3 - 1))
//
in
  loop(grid, 0, 0)
end // end of [hit_point_neighbors]
//
')

The positions out of the grid are skipped, which the typechecker makes sure for
each access to the matrix (the nested tests are needed for the constraints on
the indices to be known).

")

#para("\

It should be clear that the following function
performs one round of the aforementioned experiment:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// A point is added to the list of its cell
//
fun
theGrid_add_point
(
grid: !matrixptr(points, N, N), i: int, j: int, p: point
) : void = let
//
val i = g1ofg0(i) and j = g1ofg0(j)
//
in
//
if
(i >= 0)
then if
(i < N)
then if
(j >= 0)
then if
(j < N)
then let
  var ps: points = list_vt_nil()
  val () = matrixptr_exch_at_int(grid, i, N, j, ps)
  val () = ps := list_vt_cons(p, ps)
  val () = matrixptr_exch_at_int(grid, i, N, j, ps)
  val-~list_vt_nil() = ps
in
  // nothing
end // end of [then]
else () else () else () else () // the position is not valid
//
end // end of [theGrid_add_point]
//

//
fun
do_one
(
grid: !matrixptr(points, N, N)
) : int = let
//
val x =
N * randfloat()
val y =
N * randfloat()
//
val p = @(x, y)
//
val i =
g0float2int_double_int(x)
val j =
g0float2int_double_int(y)
//
val nhit =
hit_point_neighbors(p, grid, i, j)
//
val () = theGrid_add_point(grid, i, j, p)
//
in
  nhit
end // end of [do_one]
//
')

Note that the function #dyncode("randfloat") returns a randomly
chosen floating point number between 0 and 1 (based on the uniform
distribution). The integer returned by a call to
#dyncode("do_one") records the number of hits generated
by the point chosen at one specific round. In order to
perform K rounds of experiment, the following function can be called:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
fun
do_all
(
grid: !matrixptr(points, N, N), K: int
) : int = let
//
fun
loop
(
grid: !matrixptr(points, N, N), i: int, res: int
) : int =
  if i < K then loop(grid, i+1, do_one(grid) + res) else res
//
in
  loop(grid, 0, 0)
end // end of [do_all]
//
')

The integer returned by a call to #dyncode("do_all(grid, K)") is the tally
of the numbers of hits recorded during rounds n for n ranging from 1
to K (the loop is a tail-recursive function, which needs no closure).

")

#para("\

The grid is created and used in the function #dyncode("main0"), and the
lists of the cells are freed at the end by calling #dyncode("matrixptr_freelin"),
whose template parameter frees the content of a cell:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
implement
matrix_uninitize$clear<points>(i, j, x) = list_vt_free<point>(x)
//

implement
main0() = {
//
val () = srand48(0L)
//
val theGrid = matrixptr_tabulate<points>(i2sz(N), i2sz(N))
//
val total = do_all(theGrid, N*N)
val () =
println! ("do_all(N*N)/(N*N) = ", g0int2float_int_double(total) / (N*N), " (pi/2 = ", 3.14159265/2, ")")
//
val () = matrixptr_freelin<points>(theGrid, i2sz(N), i2sz(N))
//
} (* end of [main0] *)
')

")

#para("\

It can be proven that #dyncode("do_all(grid, N*N)/(N*N)") converges
to pi/2 (with probability 1) as N approaches the positive infinity.
(For N = 1024 the program prints a value close to 1.567, while
pi/2 is about 1.5708.)
Please find #mycodelink("LECTURE/08-10/CODE", "on-line") the entirety of
the code used in this chapter. It was compiled with #command("patscc")
(no flag is needed) and run with the address sanitizer, which reports no
leak. \

")#comment("para")

</chapter><!--id="lecture08-10"-->

